capability router/value  a router: a value holding routes, built by functions and passed along
  heroes  web.Router, web.new_router
  go      echo.New, echo.Echo, gin.New, gin.Default, gin.Engine, chi.NewRouter, chi.NewMux, chi.Mux, chi.Router, net/http.NewServeMux, net/http.ServeMux, net/http.DefaultServeMux
  rust    -
  python  flask.Flask, flask.Flask.url_map, werkzeug.routing.Map, werkzeug.routing.Rule
  note    a value and not a server: the router answers which route takes a request, and the program calls its own function for it

capability route/add  a route by method and pattern, named
  heroes  web.add
  go      echo.Echo.GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, HEAD, OPTIONS, CONNECT, TRACE, Add, echo.Route.Name, gin.RouterGroup.GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, HEAD, OPTIONS, Handle, chi.Router.Get, Post, Put, Patch, Delete, Head, Options, Connect, Trace, Method, MethodFunc, net/http.ServeMux.Handle, HandleFunc, net/http.Handle, net/http.HandleFunc
  rust    -
  python  flask.Flask.route, flask.Flask.add_url_rule, flask.Flask.get, post, put, patch, delete, werkzeug.routing.Map.add
  note    one function and the method as a value (`http.METHOD_GET`), where Echo writes one function a method: `get` is `http`'s client call, and one name keeps one meaning

capability route/any  a route answering every method
  heroes  web.add_any
  go      echo.Echo.Any, gin.RouterGroup.Any, chi.Router.Handle, chi.Router.HandleFunc, a net/http.ServeMux pattern without a method
  rust    -
  python  flask.Flask.route with every method named
  note    a route of one method wins over a route of every method on the same path, as Go's more specific pattern does

capability route/methods  one route for several methods
  heroes  web.add, once a method
  go      echo.Echo.Match, gin.RouterGroup.Match
  rust    -
  python  flask.Flask.route(methods=[...])
  note    `add` once a method, one name for them all; a name may stand on one pattern only, so `path_for` has one answer

capability route/param  a segment of the path named as a parameter, `/users/:id`
  heroes  web.param_of, web.Found
  go      echo.Context.Param, ParamNames, ParamValues, gin.Context.Param, gin.Params, gin.Params.Get, ByName, chi.URLParam, chi.URLParamFromCtx, chi.RouteParams, net/http.Request.PathValue, net/http.Request.SetPathValue
  rust    -
  python  flask.Request.view_args, werkzeug.routing.UnicodeConverter
  note    a parameter matches one segment that is not empty, unescaped: `/users/a%2Fb` gives `a/b`, as Go 1.27 answers (observed); `/users/` matches no `/users/:id` (observed in Go)

capability route/wildcard  the rest of a path named, `/files/*path`
  heroes  web.add (`*name`), web.param_of
  go      echo's `*` and echo.Context.Param("*"), gin's `*name`, chi's `*` and URLParam("*"), net/http's `{name...}`
  rust    -
  python  werkzeug.routing.PathConverter, `<path:name>`
  note    it matches the empty rest too, `/files/` and `/files` both giving "", where Go answers `/files` 307 to `/files/` (observed); every segment unescaped and joined by `/`

capability route/priority  which of several routes takes a path
  heroes  web.route
  go      echo's and gin's order (static, then parameter, then wildcard), chi's, net/http.ServeMux's most specific pattern
  rust    -
  python  werkzeug.routing.Rule's weights
  note    segment by segment from the left, a static segment before a parameter before a wildcard, and back to the next where the rest does not match: `/users/new` before `/users/:id` (observed in Go); then a route of the request's method, then `GET` for `HEAD`, then a route of every method

