capability serve/health  health, liveness and readiness endpoints answered from the program's own checks
  heroes  web.health_response, web.Check
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-17, from the community census; none of the three frameworks the census read (Echo, Flask, werkzeug) offers it. The program runs its checks with its own state, as it dispatches its routes, and hands what they found; the answer is 200 or 503 and never cached. Text is Kubernetes's: `ok`, or `[+]name ok` and `[-]name failed: reason` a line each and `health check passed` or `failed`, with `?verbose` listing every check and a failure's reason withheld unless the caller trusts the client. JSON is the IETF draft "Health Check Response Format for HTTP APIs" (draft-inadarei-api-health-check-06), `application/health+json`, chosen where `Accept` weighs it or `application/json` above text

capability webhook/standard  webhooks signed and verified by the Standard Webhooks specification
  heroes  web.sign_webhook, web.verify_webhook, web.webhook_signature, web.new_webhook_secret, web.default_webhook_tolerance
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-17, from the community census; standardwebhooks.com, the form Svix, Resend, Clerk and others send. HMAC-SHA256 over `id.timestamp.payload`, keyed by the bytes of a `whsec_` and base 64 secret, written `v1,` and standard base 64; several signatures in the field, parted by spaces, any one matching; several secrets, any one signing, so a secret rotates; a timestamp refused more than the tolerance either way, 300 s as the specification's libraries allow, its edge itself accepted. The official libraries' test vector (`msg_p5jXN8AQM9LWM0D4loKWxJek`, 1614265330, `v1,g0hM9SsE+OTPJTGt/tmIKtSyZlE3uFJELVlNIOLJ1OE=`) was computed again with Python's hmac and base64 on this machine and is a test. Its key, `whsec_MfKQ9r8GKYqrTwjUPD8ILPZIo2LaLaSw`, is the example of the standard-webhooks/standard-webhooks repository's README.md (line 60) and of its extension `spec/extensions/endpoint-verification.md`, and the id, timestamp and signature stand in the tests of its Go, Python, Ruby, PHP, JavaScript, C# and Java libraries; the specification's own text, `spec/standard-webhooks.md`, holds none of them (read through GitHub's API 2026-11-18, the test names said "the specification's example" until then). A signature of the asymmetric version `v1a` is passed over, as the next block says

